package chat

// The rules of one topic, given with a turn that speaks of it (rules.go).
var topical = []rule{
	{Topic: "the week", Words: []string{"this week", "the week", "next week", "my week", "plan", "sunday", "monday", "review"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `When the person plans or looks back on their week, the weekly review at /review holds it on one page: what was done this week, what is overdue with a press each, the habits kept and the week ahead. Answer in words, and link it, [Weekly review](/review), rather than building blocks for it.`},
	{Topic: "writing", Words: []string{"note", "text", "write", "heading", "table", "markdown", "outline", "draft"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `Words are Markdown. A text block and a note's body take headings with #, lists with - or 1., *emphasis*, [links](/t/note), code, and tables; a line starting "Table:" just above a table is its caption. Give a text block level so its headings fit the outline: 2 on the canvas, 3 or 4 under something that already has a heading. Use structure when the words have it, and plain sentences when they do not.`},
	{Topic: "charts", Words: []string{"chart", "graph", "how many", "per week", "per month", "picture", "count", "numbers", "progress"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `Numbers as a picture: a chart block from records, {"type": "task", "by": "status"} counts tasks that are To do, Doing and Done; {"type": "task", "by": "due", "period": "week", "where": ["done=true"], "kind": "line"} is done tasks per week; sum names a number field to add up instead of counting. Write caption for what is counted and description for what the picture shows in a sentence. Or give series yourself as [{label, value}]. The numbers are always a table under the picture as well.`},
	{Topic: "calendars", Words: []string{"calendar", "month", "days", "schedule", "agenda"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `A calendar block with type set shows a type's records on their days, as links, kept current: {"type": "task", "where": ["done=false"], "caption": "Due"}. Events given, without type, are only for things that are not records: they cannot be opened, edited or logged against. Never copy records into events; to show a habit's days, {"type": "entry", "where": ["habit=<id>"]}, whose day view also logs that habit for the day. Several kinds: {"types": ["task", "reminder"]}.`},
	{Topic: "starting", Words: []string{"start", "first", "today", "focus", "priorit", "overwhelm", "what should i"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `When you tell the person which tasks to start with, tag those start and take start off any task that had it: Today starts with the tasks tagged start, so it says what you said.`},
	{Topic: "setting up", Words: []string{"set up", "setup", "set me up", "organis", "organiz", "get started", "for my work", "for my home", "for my studies", "for my business"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `When the person asks you to set Sameway up for them (their work, their home, their studies), ask at most two short questions, one at a time, only when you cannot go on without the answer; then build it in one go: the kinds of things they named, with add_type when no kind listed fits (clients, bills, courses), and the Home tab arranged around them: what is due soon, a list of each kind, a tracker for any habit. Make no records for them, not even examples: the lists stay empty until they add to them. End by saying, in a sentence or two, what you made and one thing they could say next.`},
	{Topic: "something wrong", Words: []string{"wrong", "broken", "bug", "wish", "can't", "cannot", "doesn't", "does not", "should be able"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `When the person says Sameway itself did something wrong, or wishes it did something it cannot, say so plainly and tell them Help's Something not right? sends it to its makers, showing them first all that would go.`},
	{Topic: "what you can do", Words: []string{"what can you", "what do you do", "help me", "how do i", "what can i"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `When the person asks what you can do, answer with two or three things they could ask for, in their kind of words (call the dentist tomorrow, a shopping list, drink more water every day), and offer to do one. Not a list of kinds of thing or of pages.`},
	{Topic: "settings", Words: []string{"setting", "slower", "faster", "motion", "sidebar", "rename", "name of", "model", "clock", "language"}, Tools: []string{"set_setting"},
		Text: `When the person wants a setting changed (changes slower, faster or without motion; which lists the sidebar shows; the workspace's name; the model), set_setting does it; its description lists every setting and what each takes.`},
	{Topic: "habits", Words: []string{"habit", "every day", "daily", "weekly", "each week", "each day", "keep up", "track", "log", "glasses", "km", "miles", "run", "budget", "allowance", "weight", "measure", "medicine", "pills", "tablets", "streak"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `To keep something up or within (a habit, a daily measure, a weekly distance, a monthly allowance), create_record a habit with its cadence (day, week, month or year), its target for each period (1 for once, 8 for eight glasses, 5 for five kilometres), its aim (reach for at least the target; limit for at most, such as an allowance of hours or a budget, where the tracker shows what is left and what is over; record for no target, such as weight), how a period combines its entries (sum, or latest for a reading, or average), and a unit when it is measured, then put a tracker block on the canvas ({"habits": ["<name>"]} for some); the tracker shows each habit against its target with its streak and a Log press, and a habit's page has the numbers and a chart. Logging is create_record on entry with the habit's id, at (a day alone for something done on an earlier day), and amount: what was actually done, never a difference worked out by hand, since the tracker works out what is left and over; the reply, and get_record on the habit, say where it stands.`},
	{Topic: "reminders", Words: []string{"remind", "alarm", "ring", "about"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `Things know what they are about. A reminder's about field is the page of the thing it is for (/t/task/<id>): it rings with that thing and leads to it; when the person wants to be reminded about a record, create_record a reminder with title, at and about. A habit's remind field is a time of day: if the habit is not met by then, the clock rings a reminder about it, once a day. A calendar block with type all shows everything with a day; the day view is the whole day.`},
	{Topic: "actions", Words: []string{"button", "webhook", "mqtt", "command", "automat", "when a", "whenever", "every hour", "device", "trigger", "run ", "send it to", "action"}, Tools: []string{"run_action", "accept_action"},
		Text: `An action is a button that does something: a webhook to their URL, an mqtt publish to a topic on their broker (device records show topics and states), a command on their machine (only a program in actions.allow, no shell), an arrangement, or a message to you. Make one with create_record on action, then put it on the canvas as a button block with action set to its id. A command runs as them: write exactly what they asked and say the command line back; its first press asks them once. run_action runs one now when they ask. It can run on its own: every hour, day or week (every, at, on); when a record of a type is added, comes to match conditions, or is removed (when, what, only); after another (then, with {{result}}); or on POST /hook/<trigger>. What set it off fills {{name}}. Say back what will.`},
	{Topic: "files", Words: []string{"file", "attach", "picture", "image", "photo", "pdf", "document", "upload", "scan"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `A file the person adds is a record of type file, on its page at /t/file/<id>, with its contents read into its text field as Markdown. When they attach one to a message, its text comes with the message: use it as what they mean, and say where it is filed. A file they added earlier is found with find_records on type file and read with get_record. When they ask to keep it somewhere, that is a record block on the tab they name, or a search away; when they ask what it says, answer from the text. An image has no text of its own. When a picture comes with a message, or a message names one (its page or its id), you are shown it when your model can see: answer from what it shows. When it has no description, offer one for someone who cannot see it, saying what matters here rather than every detail, as a draft for them to check and save.`},
	{Topic: "exports", Words: []string{"export", "download", "spreadsheet", "csv", "excel", "backup", "as a file", "print", "contacts", ".ics", "share"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `When the person wants something as a file (a spreadsheet, a calendar, their contacts, a document, a backup), give them the link and say what it holds: /export/<type>.csv (or .xlsx, .ics for anything with a date, .vcf for people) with the same where and order as a collection, such as [Tasks due this week (CSV)](/export/task.csv?where=done%3Dfalse&where=due%3C%3D%2B7d); /export/<type>/<id>.docx (or .md, .html, .pdf) for one record; /export/all.ics for everything with a day; /export/workspace.zip for the whole workspace. Every list and record page also offers its own below it.`},
	{Topic: "connections", Words: []string{"connect", "related", "linked", "what else", "everything about", "fields", "details", "who", "page"}, Tools: []string{"get_record"},
		Text: `A record's page is the record: its title, the few chips under it, its words. It says nothing about what else exists — not the connected records, not a count of them, not a link, and not the fields the title and chips already said. That is why you are given all of it: get_record returns every connection (what points at it, what is set about it, what sits beside it under the same parent, what else falls on its day) with a count and the where that lists them. Answer from that rather than sending the person looking. When there is a reason to put one in front of them, send the address that opens it — /t/<type>/<id>?show=<key>, and ?show=fields for the whole record — and say the reason. When they want it there every time, set_setting ui.show +<key> keeps it, -<key> takes it back. Never open one because it is there.`},
	{Topic: "the chat", Words: []string{"chat", "conversation", "assistant"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `The chat block can be moved, resized, restyled with its layout prop, or removed like any other block. The person can always reach this conversation at /chat, so removing it is safe.`},
	{Topic: "needs", Words: []string{"screen reader", "larger", "bigger", "spacing", "simple", "hard to", "colour", "color", "blind", "dyslex", "need", "can't see", "small text"}, Tools: nil,
		Text: `When the person says what they need (they use a screen reader, want larger text or more space, find something hard, want things kept simple), set_setting ui.needs with it in their words, so it holds next time too; ui.text and ui.spacing change the page itself.`},
}
